 The old range default, `coherent-source-v1`, `captureLegacyCanonicalSource`, and legacy frontier contracts remain unchanged. In particular `legacyCanonicalCoverage` remains Held rather than treating a selected source receipt as a complete frontier. The new profile shares intrinsic body review while requiring fresh source-specific applicability and independent interfaces. Current full-corpus/global interface size and conservative recapture on substantive membership changes remain explicit limits; no actual resumption or semantic acceptance follows from planner metadata.
 
 The unreleased corpus experiment now supports an exact frozen semantic partition proposal and metadata-only workload inspection; see the scoped-context documentation. Complete fields of canonical JSON STRING views and recognized `{path, content|body}` file entries may become linked sections. The enclosing fields, unknown keys, qualifier links and ordered structure remain exact; decoded values remain their original types and immutable raw bytes round-trip. No text serialization ranges are semantic cuts. An indivisible 1 MB body still holds under the existing cap. These field cuts supersede the earlier experimental coherent plan layout, not published range proofs or issued company receipts.
+
+Field discovery traverses nested objects and arrays, including canonical STRING array roots and nested `sources` maps. Shared structures are discovered once, while every physical occurrence retains its own path, ancestor qualifiers and independent reconciliation obligations. Primitive members and empty structures remain in the exact ordered graph and local sections. File-path keys and small size confer no semantic independence. Discovery uses an iterative visited traversal; codec protections and opaque terminal-string holds remain. Bounded intrinsic sections do not establish bounded domain/interface requests or semantic acceptance; see the synthetic measurements and focused Node 22/25 logs in [nested-field repair report](../evals/coherent-nested-fields/report.md).
